  "worked_solution": "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+\u0394x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u03c4}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+\u0394y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is $y(x)={x}^{3}.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394y & =({x}^{2}+{y}_{\\text{exact}}(x))\\cdot \u0394x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\cdot \u0394x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+\u0394x) \\\\  & =(x+\u0394x)^{3} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot \u0394x+3x\\cdot (\u0394x)^{2}+(\u0394x)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u03c4}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot \u0394x+3x\\cdot (\u0394x)^{2}+(\u0394x)^{3}]-[{x}^{3}+({x}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\cdot \u0394x] \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{3}+(3{x}^{2}-({x}^{2}+{x}^{3}))\\cdot \u0394x+3x\\cdot (\u0394x)^{2}+(\u0394x)^{3} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\u0394x+3x(\u0394x)^{2}+(\u0394x)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
